WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:07.377
Sockets are one of the lowest-level ways C++ applications communicate across a network or between local processes.

2
00:00:07.377 --> 00:00:14.344
They appear in servers, clients, agents, embedded devices, desktop applications, service integrations, telemetry collectors, and internal tools.

3
00:00:14.344 --> 00:00:23.770
Because they sit close to the operating system and close to raw input, small mistakes can have wide effects on reliability, availability, and

4
00:00:23.770 --> 00:00:24.180
confidentiality.

5
00:00:24.180 --> 00:00:26.639
A socket is a trust boundary.

6
00:00:26.639 --> 00:00:33.607
The application may receive data from another machine, another process, another user, or an unreliable network path.

7
00:00:33.607 --> 00:00:42.213
That data might be incomplete, delayed, malformed, duplicated, unexpectedly large, or simply inconsistent with the protocol the developer had in mind.

8
00:00:42.213 --> 00:00:50.410
Secure socket programming begins by assuming that received bytes are untrusted until the protocol has framed, parsed, and validated them.

9
00:00:50.410 --> 00:00:58.197
Unsafe socket code can corrupt application state, leak resources, hang threads, expose sensitive data, accept malformed input, or weaken

10
00:00:58.197 --> 00:00:59.836
authentication and encryption decisions.

11
00:00:59.836 --> 00:01:02.705
The problem is rarely one dramatic bug.

12
00:01:02.705 --> 00:01:10.902
More often, it is a chain of ordinary engineering shortcuts: unclear protocol assumptions, manual cleanup scattered through error paths, missing

13
00:01:10.902 --> 00:01:16.230
timeouts, casual logging, or a connection lifecycle that was not designed for failure.

14
00:01:16.230 --> 00:01:18.279
The defensive mindset is practical.

15
00:01:18.279 --> 00:01:26.475
Define the protocol contract, choose exposure deliberately, represent socket ownership clearly, handle partial I/O, set limits, validate inputs, use

16
00:01:26.475 --> 00:01:30.164
approved secure-channel libraries, and make failure behavior predictable.

17
00:01:30.164 --> 00:01:39.590
Socket code deserves security review because it often becomes part of the application boundary, even when the service is intended only for internal

18
00:01:39.590 --> 00:01:40.000
use.
